      <p>Explore Zanzibar's UNESCO World Heritage Stone Town with a licensed local guide. This historic district features:</p>
      <p>Stone Town is a living museum where every alleyway tells a story of Swahili sultanates, Arab traders, German colonizers, and British administrators. A walking tour is the best way to experience its layered history, intricate architecture, and vibrant local culture. Below is a comprehensive guide to self-guided exploration, though nothing replaces the insights of a certified guide who can bring the stones to life.</p>

      <h2>Recommended Walking Routes</h2>

      <h3>Route A: Heritage Highlights (2–3 hours)</h3>
      <p>This route covers Stone Town's most iconic sites within the compact west-central quarter. Start early (08:00–09:00) to avoid heat and crowds.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Hamamni Persian Baths:</strong> Built in the 1880s by Sultan Said Majid, these public baths showcase Zanzibar's Omani engineering heritage. The underground chambers and ornamental arches are fascinating. <em>Entry: $5, open 09:00–17:00.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Sultan's Palace Museum:</strong> The former palace of Sultan Khaled bin Bargash, displaying royal regalia, Zanzibari ceremonial objects, and the famous wooden doors. <em>Entry: $6, open 09:00–17:30.</em></li>
        <li><strong>House of Wonders (Beit al-Ajaib):</strong> East Africa's first building with electricity and elevator (1889). Now a museum of Swahili culture and maritime history. <em>Entry: $6, open 09:00–18:00.</em></li>
        <li><strong>St. Joseph's Cathedral:</strong> The towering white cathedral at the heart of the Anglican cathedral complex, built in 1893 on the site of the former slave market. <em>Free entry, open 06:00–18:00.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Forodhani Gardens:</strong> The seafront gardens that come alive at night with the famous Forodhani Night Market. By day, relax on the Indian Ocean views and watch local fishermen repair nets. <em>Free, open all day.</em></li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Route B: Cultural Deep Dive (3–4 hours)</h3>
      <p>Venture into less-touristed corners of Stone Town for a more intimate experience.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Darajani Market:</strong> Zanzibar's main bustling food market, where vendors sell everything from fresh seafood to spices, fruits, and vegetables. <em>Best visited 07:00–10:00 when the market is most active.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Old Fort (Ngome Kongwe):</strong> The 17th-century Portuguese fort that now houses craft shops, live music performances, and the Zanzibar Cultural Centre. <em>Entry: $2, open 10:00–22:00.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Arabic House & Old Dispensary:</strong> Restored Swahili merchant houses with intricate carved wooden facades. The Old Dispensary now hosts art galleries and souvenir shops. <em>Free to view exterior.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Philharmonic Society (Baitaraf):</strong> Zanzibar's oldest social club, built in 1905 for the elite. Its grand ballroom and courtyard are architectural gems. <em>Exterior viewing only.</em></li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Practical Information</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Licensed guides:</strong> Expect to pay $25–40 for a 2–3 hour guided tour. Always confirm the guide is certified by the Zanzibar Commission for Tourism. <em>Ask your hotel or look for the official badge.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Best time:</strong> Early morning (08:00–11:00) for cooler temperatures and fewer crowds. Sunset walks (17:00–19:00) are magical but bring a flashlight for dimmer alleys.</li>
        <li><strong>What to wear:</strong> Comfortable walking shoes, modest clothing (knees and shoulders should be covered when visiting religious sites), and a hat/sunscreen.</li>
        <li><strong>Photography:</strong> Carry a camera or smartphone, but be respectful—ask permission before photographing people, especially inside homes and mosques.</li>
        <li><strong>Accessibility:</note> Stone Town's cobblestone streets and uneven pavements can be challenging for mobility aids. Plan routes accordingly.</em></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Historical Context</h2>
      <p>Stone Town's architecture reflects its position as a crossroads of Indian Ocean trade for over a millennium. The iconic carved wooden doors—each unique—signal the owner's wealth, religion, and social status. The Sultan's Palace and House of Wonders represent the Omani Sultanate's peak influence (19th century). The cathedral stands on the site of the last legal slave market in the Zanzibar Archipelago, which was closed in 1873 under British pressure. The Darajani Market has fed Zanzibaris since the 1830s. Understanding this history transforms a walk through narrow alleys into a journey through centuries of trade, colonization, resistance, and cultural fusion.</p>

      <h2>Self-Guided vs. Guided Tour</h2>
      <p>While Stone Town is walkable and signage exists at major sites, a licensed guide provides essential context: historical anecdotes, cultural nuances, and access to stories you'd miss alone. If you choose self-guided, download a map beforehand (cell service can be spotty in the labyrinthine quarter) and carry printed information about each site's history and entry fees.</p>

      <h2>Nearby Extensions</h2>
      <p>After your walking tour, extend your experience with these nearby attractions:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Safari Blue:</strong> A half-day dhow sailing trip from Stone Town's marina (combo tickets often available).</li>
        <li><strong>Spice Farm Tour:</strong> Just outside Stone Town, learn about Zanzibar's famous spice trade.</li>
        <li><strong>Ngome Kongwe (Old Fort):</strong> Evening cultural performances and live music.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Whether you explore independently or with a guide, Stone Town's atmospheric streets, aromatic spices, and historic grandeur make it the cultural heart of Zanzibar—an essential experience for any visitor.</p>
